# Reconstruction of mapbox/earcut.hpp — failure‑recovery + hole machinery

**Provenance / confidence.** The binary `/workspace/dataset/test19_executable` is **not stripped** and was compiled with `-O2` but with full C++ symbol names. I disassembled every relevant function and decoded it instruction‑by‑instruction, so everything below marked ✔ is **verified against the actual machine code**, not recalled. Addresses: `cureLocalIntersections@0x402970`, `splitEarcut@0x404090`, `intersects@0x4017c0`, `onSegment@0x401650`, `locallyInside@0x4016b0`, `filterPoints@0x401950`, `splitPolygon@0x403670`, `earcutLinked@0x404460`, `isEarHashed@0x4023e0`, `zOrder@0x4021f0`, `indexCurve@0x402280`, `eliminateHoles@0x403ae0`, `linkedList@0x4034d0`, `operator()@0x4047d0`. (`isEar`, `isValidDiagonal`, `intersectsPolygon`, `middleInside`, `area`, `equals`, `sign`, `pointInTriangle`, `removeNode`, `sortLinked`, `findHoleBridge`, `eliminateHole`, `getLeftmost`, `sectorContainsSector` were inlined into their callers and recovered from the inlined code.)

**Version verdict:** this is the **v2.2.4-era** header (C++ port of earcut.js 2.2.4). Evidence: `sectorContainsSector` is present and inlined into `findHoleBridge`; `eliminateHole` uses the `bridgeReverse` form; `intersects` is the `sign()`/`onSegment` version; `earcutLinked(Node*, int pass)`; `zOrder(double,double)`; 80‑point hashing threshold.

**One deviation from upstream — the `Node` layout.** The header used here has a *repacked* 56‑byte `Node` (upstream is 64 bytes with `i` first). This is behaviourally irrelevant but explains the `and $0x7fffffff` masks all over the disassembly:

```cpp
struct Node {
    Node(N index, double x_, double y_) : x(x_), y(y_), i(index) {}
    Node(const Node&) = delete;  Node& operator=(const Node&) = delete;
    Node(Node&&) = delete;       Node& operator=(Node&&) = delete;

    const double x;            // 0x00
    const double y;            // 0x08
    Node* prev    = nullptr;   // 0x10
    Node* next    = nullptr;   // 0x18
    int32_t z     = 0;         // 0x20
    N    i     : 31;           // 0x24 bits 0..30
    bool steiner : 1;          // 0x24 bit 31   (tested as sign bit of byte 0x27)
    Node* prevZ   = nullptr;   // 0x28
    Node* nextZ   = nullptr;   // 0x30
};                             // sizeof == 56
```
For a port, treat it as plain `i: u32` + `steiner: bool`. Indices are emitted as `indices.emplace_back(node->i)` into a `std::vector<N>`.

---

## 0. Primitives (all ✔ verified)

```cpp
// area of a triangle; sign gives orientation
double area(const Node* p, const Node* q, const Node* r) const {
    return (q->y - p->y) * (r->x - q->x) - (q->x - p->x) * (r->y - q->y);
}

bool equals(const Node* p1, const Node* p2) {
    return p1->x == p2->x && p1->y == p2->y;
}

// for collinearity test in `intersects`
template <typename T> int sign(T val) {
    return (T(0) < val) - (val < T(0));
}

bool pointInTriangle(double ax, double ay, double bx, double by,
                     double cx, double cy, double px, double py) const {
    return (cx - px) * (ay - py) >= (ax - px) * (cy - py) &&
           (ax - px) * (by - py) >= (bx - px) * (ay - py) &&
           (bx - px) * (cy - py) >= (cx - px) * (by - py);
}

void removeNode(Node* p) {
    p->next->prev = p->prev;
    p->prev->next = p->next;
    if (p->prevZ) p->prevZ->nextZ = p->nextZ;
    if (p->nextZ) p->nextZ->prevZ = p->prevZ;
}
```
Note `area`'s exact operand order — it is *not* the textbook cross product about `p`; it is about `q`. Reproduce it literally or you will get different floating‑point results on degenerate/near‑degenerate input, which this self‑intersecting polygon hits constantly.

`filterPoints` (✔ verified, needed because both recovery paths call it):

```cpp
Node* filterPoints(Node* start, Node* end = nullptr) {
    if (!end) end = start;

    Node* p = start;
    bool again;
    do {
        again = false;

        if (!p->steiner && (equals(p, p->next) || area(p->prev, p, p->next) == 0)) {
            removeNode(p);
            p = end = p->prev;

            if (p == p->next) break;
            again = true;

        } else {
            p = p->next;
        }
    } while (again || p != end);

    return end;
}
```

---

## 1. `cureLocalIntersections` ✔ verified byte-for-byte

```cpp
// go through all polygon nodes and cure small local self-intersections
template <typename N>
typename Earcut<N>::Node*
Earcut<N>::cureLocalIntersections(Node* start) {
    Node* p = start;
    do {
        Node* a = p->prev;
        Node* b = p->next->next;

        // a, p, p->next, b
        if (!equals(a, b) && intersects(a, p, p->next, b) &&
            locallyInside(a, b) && locallyInside(b, a)) {

            indices.emplace_back(a->i);
            indices.emplace_back(p->i);
            indices.emplace_back(b->i);

            // remove two nodes involved
            removeNode(p);
            removeNode(p->next);

            p = start = b;
        }
        p = p->next;
    } while (p != start);

    return filterPoints(p);
}
```

**Exact emission order: `a->i`, then `p->i`, then `b->i`** — i.e. `prev`, `current`, `next->next`. Verified at `0x402a38/0x402a60/0x402a7f` (three sequential `push_back`s reading `0x24(%r13)=a`, `0x24(%rbp)=p`, `0x24(%r12)=b`).

Critical control-flow details that a naive port gets wrong:

* The guard order is exactly `!equals(a,b)` → `intersects(a,p,p->next,b)` → `locallyInside(a,b)` → `locallyInside(b,a)`, **short-circuited**. `intersects` is only called if `!equals(a,b)`.
* `removeNode(p)` happens **before** `removeNode(p->next)`, and `p->next` is re-read *after* `p` was unlinked (the compiler reloads `0x18(%rbp)` at `0x402ace`). Because `removeNode(p)` does not modify `p`'s own `prev`/`next` fields, `p->next` is still the original successor — but the *second* `removeNode` then operates on a node whose `prev` has already been rewired to `a`. Net effect: both `p` and `p->next` leave the ring, `a->next == b`, `b->prev == a`.
* After the cure, `p = start = b;` and then the loop-tail `p = p->next` runs, so the next node examined is `b->next` and the loop terminates when it wraps back to `b`.
* The return is `filterPoints(p)` with the **default `end = nullptr`** (asm: `xor %esi,%esi`), and at loop exit `p == start`, so `filterPoints(start, nullptr)` is equivalent. `filterPoints` is a tail call.

---

## 2. `splitEarcut` ✔ verified byte-for-byte

```cpp
// try splitting polygon into two and triangulate them independently
template <typename N>
void Earcut<N>::splitEarcut(Node* start) {
    // look for a valid diagonal that divides the polygon into two
    Node* a = start;
    do {
        Node* b = a->next->next;
        while (b != a->prev) {
            if (a->i != b->i && isValidDiagonal(a, b)) {
                // split the polygon in two by the diagonal
                Node* c = splitPolygon(a, b);

                // filter colinear points around the cuts
                a = filterPoints(a, a->next);
                c = filterPoints(c, c->next);

                // run earcut on each half
                earcutLinked(a);
                earcutLinked(c);
                return;
            }
            b = b->next;
        }
        a = a->next;
    } while (a != start);
}
```

Verified specifics:

* `a->i != b->i` lives **in `splitEarcut`**, not in `isValidDiagonal` (asm at `0x4040e4`, before the two `isValidDiagonal` index tests at `0x4040f4` and `0x404106`).
* Inner loop is `while (b != a->prev)`, with `a->prev` loaded once per outer iteration (`%r12`).
* Order after a successful diagonal: `splitPolygon(a,b)` → `filterPoints(a, a->next)` → `filterPoints(c, c->next)` → `earcutLinked(a, 0)` → `earcutLinked(c, 0)` → `return`. Both recursive calls pass `pass = 0` (asm `xor %edx,%edx`), so **z-order hashing is re-indexed inside each half** if `hashing` is set. The second `earcutLinked` is a tail call.
* `a` and `c` are **reassigned** from the `filterPoints` results before being passed on. Do not pass the pre-filter pointers.
* Returns `void`.

---

## 3. `isValidDiagonal` — your version is CORRECT ✔

Confirmed exactly, including operand order and the fact that `(area(...) != 0 || area(...) != 0)` is compiled as two `ucomisd`-against-zero tests:

```cpp
// check if a diagonal between two polygon nodes is valid (lies in polygon interior)
template <typename N>
bool Earcut<N>::isValidDiagonal(Node* a, Node* b) {
    return a->next->i != b->i && a->prev->i != b->i && !intersectsPolygon(a, b) && // dones't intersect other edges
           ((locallyInside(a, b) && locallyInside(b, a) && middleInside(a, b) &&   // locally visible
             (area(a->prev, a, b->prev) != 0 || area(a, b->prev, b) != 0)) ||      // does not create opposite-facing sectors
            (equals(a, b) && area(a->prev, a, a->next) > 0 && area(b->prev, b, b->next) > 0)); // special zero-length case
}
```

Verified evaluation order in the binary (short‑circuited, left to right):
1. `a->next->i != b->i` (`0x4040f4`)
2. `a->prev->i != b->i` (`0x404106`)
3. `intersectsPolygon(a, b)` (inlined loop `0x404119..0x40417b`)
4. `locallyInside(a, b)` (`0x404199`)
5. `locallyInside(b, a)` (`0x4041bf`)
6. `middleInside(a, b)` (inlined `0x4041cc..0x404287`)
7. `area(a->prev, a, b->prev) != 0` (`0x4043d6..0x40441a`)
8. `area(a, b->prev, b) != 0` (`0x404426..0x404449`)
9. fall-through to the `equals` clause: `equals(a,b)` (`0x404290`), `area(a->prev,a,a->next) > 0` (`0x4042c1..0x4042fd`), `area(b->prev,b,b->next) > 0` (`0x404303..0x40433b`)

Note the *jump structure*: if the sector test (7 ∥ 8) fails, control falls into the `equals(a,b)` clause; if `locallyInside`/`middleInside` fails it *also* jumps to the `equals` clause. That is precisely the `(A && B && C && D) || (E && F && G)` shape above.

**VARIANT B (older earcut ≤ 2.1.x / earcut.js 2.1.x)** — *not* what this binary contains, but the historical alternative:
```cpp
bool isValidDiagonal(Node* a, Node* b) {
    return a->next->i != b->i && a->prev->i != b->i &&
           !intersectsPolygon(a, b) &&
           locallyInside(a, b) && locallyInside(b, a) &&
           middleInside(a, b);
}
```

---

## 4. `intersects` — the modern header uses the `sign()` + `onSegment` version ✔

The binary contains **only** this version; there is a real `onSegment` symbol at `0x401650` called four times from `intersects`.

```cpp
// check if two segments intersect
template <typename N>
bool Earcut<N>::intersects(const Node* p1, const Node* q1, const Node* p2, const Node* q2) {
    int o1 = sign(area(p1, q1, p2));
    int o2 = sign(area(p1, q1, q2));
    int o3 = sign(area(p2, q2, p1));
    int o4 = sign(area(p2, q2, q1));

    if (o1 != o2 && o3 != o4) return true; // general case

    if (o1 == 0 && onSegment(p1, p2, q1)) return true; // p1, q1 and p2 are collinear and p2 lies on p1q1
    if (o2 == 0 && onSegment(p1, q2, q1)) return true; // p1, q1 and q2 are collinear and q2 lies on p1q1
    if (o3 == 0 && onSegment(p2, p1, q2)) return true; // p2, q2 and p1 are collinear and p1 lies on p2q2
    if (o4 == 0 && onSegment(p2, q1, q2)) return true; // p2, q2 and q1 are collinear and q1 lies on p2q2

    return false;
}
```

All four `o` values are computed **unconditionally and up front** (no short-circuit), then the general case, then the four collinear cases in that exact order. Verified: the `sign` idiom compiles to `seta %sil` / `seta %al` / `sub %eax,%esi`, i.e. `(0.0 < v) - (v < 0.0)`, which yields `0` for NaN.

**There is NO early `equals`-based short-circuit** in this build. Some newer earcut.js snapshots begin with
```js
if ((equals(p1,q1) || equals(p2,q2)) && ...) ...
```
— absent here.

**VARIANT B — the pre-2.2 simple version** (do not use for this binary; kept for reference):
```cpp
bool intersects(const Node* p1, const Node* q1, const Node* p2, const Node* q2) {
    if ((equals(p1, q1) && equals(p2, q2)) ||
        (equals(p1, q2) && equals(p2, q1))) return true;
    return (area(p1, q1, p2) > 0) != (area(p1, q1, q2) > 0) &&
           (area(p2, q2, p1) > 0) != (area(p2, q2, q1) > 0);
}
```

---

## 5. `intersectsPolygon`, `locallyInside`, `middleInside`, `onSegment`, `sign`, `sectorContainsSector`

All ✔ verified.

```cpp
// for collinear points p, q, r, check if point q lies on segment pr
template <typename N>
bool Earcut<N>::onSegment(const Node* p, const Node* q, const Node* r) {
    return q->x <= std::max<double>(p->x, r->x) &&
           q->x >= std::min<double>(p->x, r->x) &&
           q->y <= std::max<double>(p->y, r->y) &&
           q->y >= std::min<double>(p->y, r->y);
}
```
(asm uses `maxsd`/`minsd` in exactly this x-then-y order; note `maxsd a,b` semantics make the `std::max<double>(p,r)` argument order irrelevant here since no NaNs occur.)

```cpp
// check if a polygon diagonal intersects any polygon segments
template <typename N>
bool Earcut<N>::intersectsPolygon(const Node* a, const Node* b) {
    const Node* p = a;
    do {
        if (p->i != a->i && p->next->i != a->i && p->i != b->i && p->next->i != b->i &&
                intersects(p, p->next, a, b)) return true;
        p = p->next;
    } while (p != a);

    return false;
}
```
The four index guards are in **that exact order** (`p->i != a->i`, `p->next->i != a->i`, `p->i != b->i`, `p->next->i != b->i`), verified at `0x404132 / 0x40413e / 0x40414d / 0x404157`.

```cpp
// check if a polygon diagonal is locally inside the polygon
template <typename N>
bool Earcut<N>::locallyInside(const Node* a, const Node* b) {
    return area(a->prev, a, a->next) < 0 ?
        area(a, b, a->next) >= 0 && area(a, a->prev, b) >= 0 :
        area(a, b, a->prev) < 0 || area(a, a->next, b) < 0;
}
```
Verified: the discriminant is `area(a->prev, a, a->next) < 0`; the convex branch is `>= 0 && >= 0`, the reflex branch is `< 0 || < 0`. Argument orders are `(a, b, a->next)`, `(a, a->prev, b)`, `(a, b, a->prev)`, `(a, a->next, b)` — note the asymmetry (`b` is the middle argument in two of them and the last in the other two). Getting this wrong changes results on degenerate collinear cases.

```cpp
// check if the middle Vertex of a polygon diagonal is inside the polygon
template <typename N>
bool Earcut<N>::middleInside(const Node* a, const Node* b) {
    const Node* p = a;
    bool inside = false;
    double px = (a->x + b->x) / 2;
    double py = (a->y + b->y) / 2;
    do {
        if (((p->y > py) != (p->next->y > py)) && p->next->y != p->y &&
            (px < (p->next->x - p->x) * (py - p->y) / (p->next->y - p->y) + p->x))
                inside = !inside;
        p = p->next;
    } while (p != a);

    return inside;
}
```
Verified including the `p->next->y != p->y` guard (asm `ucomisd`+`setp`+`cmovne`) and the multiply-by-`0.5` form (constant `0.5` at `.rodata:0x4050a0`, i.e. `(a->x + b->x) * 0.5` — GCC turned `/2` into `*0.5`, which is exact).

```cpp
// whether sector in vertex m contains sector in vertex p in the same coordinates
template <typename N>
bool Earcut<N>::sectorContainsSector(const Node* m, const Node* p) {
    return area(m->prev, m, p->prev) < 0 && area(p->next, m, m->next) < 0;
}
```
✔ Verified inlined inside `findHoleBridge` at `0x403ec3..0x403f5d`. Its presence is the strongest single marker that this is the 2.2.4 header.

---

## 6. `splitPolygon` ✔ verified byte-for-byte

```cpp
// link two polygon vertices with a bridge; if the vertices belong to the same ring, it splits
// polygon into two; if one belongs to the outer ring and another to a hole, it merges it into a
// single ring
template <typename N>
typename Earcut<N>::Node*
Earcut<N>::splitPolygon(Node* a, Node* b) {
    Node* a2 = nodes.construct(a->i, a->x, a->y);
    Node* b2 = nodes.construct(b->i, b->x, b->y);
    Node* an = a->next;
    Node* bp = b->prev;

    a->next = b;
    b->prev = a;

    a2->next = an;
    an->prev = a2;

    b2->next = a2;
    a2->prev = b2;

    bp->next = b2;
    b2->prev = bp;

    return b2;
}
```

Exact order verified:
1. **`a2` is allocated first, then `b2`** (two separate `ObjectPool::construct` inlinings, `0x403704` then `0x403764`). This matters for any port that reproduces node identity/ordering, and for pool-block growth.
2. Both new nodes are zero-initialised: `prev = next = prevZ = nextZ = nullptr`, `z = 0`, `steiner = false`; only `x`, `y`, `i` are copied (the `x,y` pair is copied as one 16-byte `movupd`/`movups`).
3. `an = a->next` and `bp = b->prev` are read **before** any relinking (asm reads `0x18(%r13)` and `0x10(%r14)` before the six stores).
4. The six relink stores are in exactly the order shown.
5. Returns `b2`.

---

## 7. Holes — `eliminateHoles`, `eliminateHole`, `findHoleBridge`, the sort

This test has no holes (single ring), so these never fire here, but they are ✔ verified present in this build.

```cpp
template <typename N> template <typename Polygon>
typename Earcut<N>::Node*
Earcut<N>::eliminateHoles(const Polygon& points, Node* outerNode) {
    const size_t len = points.size();

    // process holes from left to right
    queue.clear();                       // `queue` is a std::vector<Node*> MEMBER of Earcut
    for (size_t i = 1; i < len; i++) {
        Node* list = linkedList(points[i], false);
        if (list) {
            if (list == list->next) list->steiner = true;
            queue.push_back(getLeftmost(list));
        }
    }
    std::sort(queue.begin(), queue.end(), [](const Node* a, const Node* b) {
        return a->x < b->x;
    });

    // process holes from left to right
    for (size_t i = 0; i < queue.size(); i++) {
        outerNode = eliminateHole(queue[i], outerNode);
    }

    return outerNode;
}
```
Verified: `queue` is cleared at entry; holes are built with `clockwise = false`; the `list == list->next → steiner = true` branch is the `orb $0x80,0x27(%rdx)` at `0x403be0`; the sort is `std::sort` with a comparator lambda (introsort + insertion sort + heap-adjust instantiations all present). `outerNode` **is** reassigned from `eliminateHole`'s return.

**`eliminateHole` — this build uses the 2.2.4 `bridgeReverse` form, and it DOES call both `filterPoints` and `splitPolygon`** (✔ verified at `0x403f80..0x403fae`):

```cpp
// find a bridge between vertices that connects hole with an outer ring and link it
template <typename N>
typename Earcut<N>::Node*
Earcut<N>::eliminateHole(Node* hole, Node* outerNode) {
    Node* bridge = findHoleBridge(hole, outerNode);
    if (!bridge) {
        return outerNode;
    }

    Node* bridgeReverse = splitPolygon(bridge, hole);

    // filter collinear points around the cuts
    filterPoints(bridgeReverse, bridgeReverse->next);
    return filterPoints(bridge, bridge->next);
